{
  "version": 2,
  "template": "quadruped",
  "space": "delta",
  "comment": "Troupe's clip library for the quadruped skeleton. Local joint rotations, XYZW. space=delta means each value is a rotation from the bone's own REST orientation, not the pose editor's node-local frame -- see blender_worker.POSE_SPACES. Every value here was computed from a world-axis angle and converted through the bone's rest frame (basis = rest^-1 * R * rest), so 'swing the left fore leg 16 degrees forward' means that about the world X axis on the template's own unit box, whatever roll Blender gives the bone. walk is a four-beat LATERAL-SEQUENCE walk -- footfalls LH, LF, RH, RF, so the four legs sit a quarter cycle apart and no two ever swing together; each key is the instant one foot strikes. run is a transverse gallop with a left lead, the right side lagging the left by a quarter beat. attack rears the whole animal onto its hind legs before the strike, which is why its apex leaves the rest bounding box vertically and why the sheet renderer frames a row from the union of its cells. root_translation is in character-height units.",
